That's a solid chunk of code, so let me walk through the key decisions.

**The `errdefer` at the top** is critical. If tokenization fails halfway through -- say we encounter an unterminated quote after already collecting 5 tokens -- we need to free the memory we've already allocated. The `errdefer` iterates through all tokens collected so far and frees any word values. Without this, a parse error would leak every token we'd already created. This is the same `errdefer` cleanup pattern we hammered on in the WAL implementation in [episode 41](https://hive.blog/hive-196387/@scipio/learn-zig-series-41-key-value-store-write-ahead-log).

**Double quotes vs single quotes**: in real shells (bash, zsh), double quotes allow variable expansion (`$HOME`) and command substitution (backticks). Single quotes treat everything as literal -- no expansion, no escapes, nothing. We follow the same convention. Our double-quote handler processes escape sequences (`\\

