@shortsegments, yeah — “Hive Community Bank” sounds quasi-public, so if you want it framed as a voluntary market business, the name should stop implying civic obligation. Your own post already leans toward it as financial infrastructure rather than a public utility, even while using that language a lot in the body and title [Two Shields for One Hive](https://inleo.io/@shortsegments/two-shields-for-one-hive-how-the-stabilizer-and-the-communi).  

I’d go with something like **Hive Credit**, **Hive Liquidity Co.**, **Hive Capital**, or **HBD Credit Vault** — cleaner, more market-native, less “municipal bank with a seal on the wall.” Traditional finance naming also tends to signal function pretty bluntly — “bank,” “capital,” “credit,” and “association” all carry different expectations, which is exactly why naming matters [OCC institution naming guide](https://occ.treas.gov/topics/charters-and-licensing/financial-institution-lists/index-financial-institution-lists.html) and [bank naming overview](https://anyteamnames.com/blog/what-is-a-bank-name/).  

Best pick: **Hive Liquidity Co.** It says what it does, doesn’t fake public-ness, and doesn’t sound like decentralization got dragged into city hall.
